# Round 4b: bolder ideas (no tools, no file edits, answer from your own knowledge)

Context: solo participant, 34 hours, RTX 4060 8GB, open data only, no wet lab, virtual hackathon (Open Scientific Intelligence, materials science and chemistry focus, criteria: impact, innovativeness, scalability, relevance). Judges ask "is two days to a real discovery possible?" Goal is FIRST PLACE. Prioritize boldness and headline impact over safety. We want the idea an expert would call the most surprising result of the hackathon, the one the organizer would retweet, still executable solo in 34 h with real data. Not dataset audits, database cleaning, or agent benchmarks.

Already considered, in rounds 1 to 4 (do not repeat): agentic discovery benchmark; EigenDrop polymorph search; hidden auxetics; SinkBreaker reaction network sinks; GNoME synthesis reality check; DataDoctor and every dataset audit variant (SpinGhost, ThermoGhost, HydrateTrap, hull reconciliation, COD audit, literature contradiction court); NIST adsorption rank reversal; diffusion compensation effect.
Round 4 ideas from the three models (all proposed, none chosen yet):
XRD twins (indistinguishable crystals by powder diffraction); hydrogen kinetic twins (two mechanisms fitting same data); message-passing GNN error floor; robust glass coating optimum; single conformer illusion for intramolecular H bonds; mathematical equivalence tests for atomistic software; HyperbolicScout (infrared permittivity tensors from phonon and Born charge databases, check against RRUFF spectra); CrysRUM (rigid unit mode and negative thermal expansion enumeration over framework crystals); AtropoScope (hidden axial chirality in ChEMBL); TopoTwin (topochemical intercalation hosts via sublattice isomorphism); AlchemGrad (differentiable alchemical MLIP over site occupancy); WignerLaw (symbolic regression of rattling thermal conductivity law); ChronoCSP (can a laptop predict crystal structures published after its training cutoff); AmorphousLimit at scale (amorphous synthesizability bound over 3 to 5 times more systems); InvariantFuzz (metamorphic testing of materials software); NoveltyCourt (how novel are generative crystal models); ConjectureBreaker (counterexamples to chemical graph theory conjectures); AntiHammond (where Bell-Evans-Polanyi fails over 170k DFT reactions).

Task: propose exactly 2 BOLDER ideas than anything above, different in kind from all of them. For each: headline, steps with hours, real open data and tools, the hard checkable result, why it is new with closest prior work, verification, main risk, 1st-place odds in percent. Then name the single boldest idea that is still feasible solo in 34 hours (from yours or from the list above) and defend it in one paragraph with a hedged feasibility estimate. Limit 700 words. No dashes as punctuation.

